-- title:
--   Proposition A.6, EC pp. 8–9 — unique efficient switched demand
-- statement:
--   Fix a good $m$, a feasible profile of unserved demands $\bar\sigma_i^m$, and nonnegative switching costs $\kappa_i^m$. There is exactly one efficient switched demand profile $\sigma_i^m$ in the sense of Definition 2.2. For every firm with $\bar\sigma_i^m>0$ it is
--
--   $$\sigma_i^m=\min\left\{\bar\sigma_i^m,\frac{\bar\sigma_i^m}{\sum_{j:\,\kappa_j^m=\kappa_i^m}\bar\sigma_j^m}\left(s_m(\kappa_i^m)-\sum_{j:\,\kappa_j^m>\kappa_i^m}\bar\sigma_j^m\right)^+\right\}.$$
--
--   The result identifies the allocation generated by the sourcing optimization and proportional tie rule.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Sums range over firms with positive unserved demand. Inactive firms receive zero. Feasible demand arises from a matrix with entries between zero and the original orders, after safety stock is subtracted and the positive part taken. The costs of active buyers are assumed nonnegative, the strategy space of Definition 3.2 (deviations $\kappa\ge0$).
-- source:
--   Birge, Capponi & Chen, Disruption and Rerouting in Supply Chain Networks, SSRN 3669363 (version of October 31, 2022; Oper. Res. 2023, DOI 10.1287/opre.2022.2409), E-Companion, pp. EC 8–9 (PDF pp. 50–51), Proposition A.6, (A.14)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DisruptReroute_GenEq_Model

namespace DisruptReroute.GenEq

/-- Proposition A.6: existence, uniqueness, and formula (A.14). -/
theorem proposition_A_6 {N M : ℕ} (net : Network N M) (m : Fin M)
    (Δ : Matrix N M) (cost : Fin N → ℝ)
    (h : Standing net) (hΔ : InBounds net Δ)
    (hc : ∀ i ∈ active N (sbar net Δ m), 0 ≤ cost i) :
    (∃! x : Fin N → ℝ, EfficientS net m (sbar net Δ m) cost x) ∧
    (∀ x : Fin N → ℝ, EfficientS net m (sbar net Δ m) cost x →
      ∀ i ∈ active N (sbar net Δ m),
        x i = greedyS net m (sbar net Δ m) cost i) := by sorry

end DisruptReroute.GenEq
